# PART 3 — MAKE THE PROJECT READY FOR REAL PORTFOLIO INTEGRATION

## Important architectural context

This current project is a standalone implementation and testing environment for the interactive portfolio experience.

After the visual and functional improvements are finished, I intend to move or merge the relevant code into my existing portfolio website.

The existing production portfolio website will be the source of truth for my actual profile information, projects, skills, certificates, social links, and feedback data.

I will connect the appropriate API or backend integration during the integration stage.

**Do not assume that the current standalone project already has a working connection to my production backend. Do not invent a backend contract or claim that the API is connected when it is not.**

The current task is to make the code clean, reusable, and integration-ready.

Do not build an entirely new production portfolio application. Improve the existing standalone experience so its components can be integrated into the original website without unnecessary rewrites.

## A. Separate the 3D experience from portfolio content

Review the current application structure and keep responsibilities clearly separated.

The intended conceptual structure is:

### 1. World and game layer

Owns the 3D environment, character, robot, camera, movement, rendering, billboards, interaction detection, and zone transitions.

It should not contain hardcoded copies of my personal information, project descriptions, contact URLs, or review text.

### 2. Portfolio presentation layer

Owns the visual presentation and interaction behavior of About, Projects, Skills, Certificates, Contact, and Feedback.

It should render the selected record or current dataset rather than duplicating the information in different components.

### 3. Data-access layer

Owns fetching, normalization, caching, and submission of portfolio data.

Continue using and improving the existing `PortfolioRepository` and data models where appropriate.

### 4. Integration boundary

Provides a clear, documented entry point for embedding the experience into the existing production website.

It should define the configuration and data contracts the host application is expected to provide.

Do not introduce an unnecessarily complicated plugin system or dependency injection framework. Keep this boundary practical and proportionate to the existing codebase.

## B. Preserve the existing portfolio zones

Keep the six main billboards and their current intended behavior:

- About Me.
- Projects.
- Skills.
- Certificates.
- Contact and Social Links.
- Feedback and Reviews.

Preserve the dedicated zones, dynamically arranged billboards, interaction prompts, return-to-home navigation, Quick View, and accessible detail panels.

Each billboard must continue to obtain its summary and action from the correct data model.

The detail view must use the corresponding real record where data exists.

A project board must open its own selected project, a social board must use its own actual destination, a certificate board must display its own record, and review pagination must use the correct page of reviews.

Do not allow visual or architectural refactoring to break these relationships.

## C. Standardize the data contract

Inspect the current portfolio types and repository.

Reuse the current schema wherever possible rather than introducing competing models.

Ensure the architecture can represent the following content:

- Profile and biography.
- Projects.
- Skills and skill categories.
- Certificates.
- Contact methods and social links.
- Published feedback and reviews.
- Feedback submission.

Use stable identifiers when records have them.

Ensure each billboard references an appropriate record or section action, rather than depending on its displayed title or array position to determine behavior.

The system must continue supporting empty states when the backend contains no records for an optional category.

Do not fabricate projects, credentials, reviews, URLs, user metrics, or personal details.

If current development data contains unverified content, keep it clearly separated from production data and document what needs verification.

## D. Keep the data source replaceable

The current project must support replacing local development data with a real API implementation without rewriting the 3D scene.

The scene should consume normalized portfolio data through the existing repository or a clearly defined equivalent.

Avoid direct network requests scattered throughout the scene components.

Do not embed API URLs or authentication logic inside `WorldScene.ts`, `HologramBillboard.ts`, or the robot component.

If the current backend configuration is missing:

- Keep the existing adapter ready for later configuration.
- Document the expected API contract only when that contract is actually defined.
- Keep development data isolated.
- Handle unavailable data honestly.
- Do not modify production backend settings or introduce fake persistence.

Use only public client-safe configuration in browser code. Never expose private keys, service-role credentials, or server secrets.

## E. Prepare a clear integration entry point

Inspect the existing `App.tsx` and the surrounding component architecture.

Provide a documented integration component or entry point that allows the original portfolio website to embed the 3D experience into an existing page or route.

Use a suitable existing component if one already serves this purpose.

The integration boundary should make it possible for the host website to supply or configure:

- The data repository or data adapter.
- The initial entry mode, such as the 3D world or Quick View.
- Optional callbacks for navigation to host-website routes, if required.
- Any other configuration genuinely necessary for integration.

Do not pass unnecessary configuration props merely for abstraction.

The embedded experience must avoid taking over the host website's global routing, document structure, or global styles.

Review the existing CSS for global selectors, conflicting styles, and overlays that might affect the rest of the production website.

Keep styles scoped appropriately. Avoid unnecessary changes to global `body`, universal selectors, or global element behavior.

Avoid introducing duplicate WebGL renderers or background scenes when mounting and unmounting the experience.

If the host website already has a common layout, data-fetching layer, or routing architecture, the integration should be able to reuse it rather than duplicate it.

## F. Document the integration procedure

Create a practical integration document, using the project's existing documentation convention or a clear new integration guide.

It should explain:

1. Which directories and files belong to the reusable 3D experience.
2. Which files contain portfolio content and data contracts.
3. Which files are specific to the standalone demo.
4. Which existing dependencies are required.
5. Which environment variables are required and whether they must be server-side or client-safe.
6. How to mount the 3D experience into an existing React page or route.
7. How to connect the original website's portfolio data or API repository.
8. How the integration should preserve the original site's routing, styles, and components.
9. Which components can be kept independent and which require adaptation.
10. How to test the integration before deployment.

Do not tell me to copy files blindly without explaining their dependencies.

Do not claim that the experience can be integrated by copying a single file if it actually depends on many modules, assets, styles, or libraries.

The purpose is to minimize the work required to integrate the experience later and make that work predictable.

